# =============================================================================
# Alerts — the control node is the single source of operator alerts.
#
# Everything here is evaluated server-side; the browser only renders what the
# server publishes (alert / alert_snapshot / alert_acked messages).
#
# Placeholders in messages are interpolated when the alert is raised:
#   {node}    the daqNode refDes            (template events)
#   {refDes}  the channel refDes            (template events; node name for
#                                            node-level events)
#   {value}   the value that tripped it     (bad_data)
#   {ANY-CHANNEL}  any channel name — its current value
#
# Severities: info | warning | alarm
# =============================================================================

# The template is instantiated once per enabled daqNode in config/daqNodes/.
# `stale` fires when a connected node stops delivering data; the optional
# duration after the event name overrides the 2000 ms default.
template every_daqnode
    on disconnect -> alarm   "{node} disconnected"
    on reconnect  -> info    "{node} reconnected"
    on bad_data   -> warning "{refDes} out of range: {value}"
    on stale 2s   -> warning "{node} data stale"

# ── Example rule ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXAMPLE ONLY — this is the reference for writing rule alerts, using channels
# that already exist in this config (CPT-01 chamber pressure vs. the operator-
# settable LIM-CPT01-HIGH abort threshold).  Rules are edge-triggered: they
# raise on false → true.  `latch` keeps the alert raised after the condition
# clears, until an operator acknowledges it — which is what you want for a
# transient over-pressure that nobody may have been watching.  Remove `latch`
# and the alert clears itself when the pressure comes back down.
alert CHAMBER-HIGH
    if CPT-01 > LIM-CPT01-HIGH
    severity alarm
    message "Chamber pressure high: {CPT-01} psia (limit {LIM-CPT01-HIGH})"
    latch
